 Give each ticket its **blocking edges**: the other tickets that must complete before it can start. A ticket with no blockers can start immediately.
 
+**Devflow additions** (always apply when the source is a devflow spec):
+
+- **Interfaces first.** When several tickets share new interfaces, the first ticket creates them: exact signatures as stubs that raise `NotImplementedError`, plus shared types. Later tickets are blocked by it, so parallel testers and implementers work against fixed interfaces without colliding. Its acceptance criteria must stay true after the later tickets land, so they pin only public import paths, names, signatures and shared types, never which private module holds an implementation. Never "raises `NotImplementedError`", which later tickets must break.
+- **Footprint.** Give each ticket a module-level footprint: the modules or packages it will touch (not file paths or line numbers).
+- **Lane.** Give each ticket a lane, `fast` or `rigor`, with a one-line reason, using the lane heuristics file the caller names (default `~/.claude/skills/run-lanes/lane-heuristics.md`). If the caller pre-fills a lane, use it for every ticket. The user can still change any lane.
+- **Overlap check.** For every pair of tickets that could run at the same time (neither blocks the other, directly or transitively), compare footprints, **whatever their lanes**. If two `fast` tickets overlap, merge the tickets, add a blocking edge, or flag the pair for the user. For a `fast`/`rigor` pair, say "safe while run-lanes alternates the lanes; serialise if they ever run concurrently". Say what you did for each overlapping pair. (**devflow auto run:** if the caller said this is a devflow auto run, follow `align`'s AUTO.md: merge or add a blocking edge, never flag.)
+- **Structural splits.** If keeping tickets parallel would need a structural change the spec didn't ask for (for example, splitting one module into a package so footprints don't overlap), don't make it silently. Say so plainly in the proposal, and let the user choose "split" or "serialise". (**devflow auto run:** if the caller said this is a devflow auto run, follow `align`'s AUTO.md: serialise, and log it.)
+
 **Wide refactors are the exception to vertical slicing.** A **wide refactor** is one mechanical change (rename a column, retype a shared symbol) whose **blast radius** fans across the whole codebase, so a single edit breaks thousands of call sites at once and no vertical slice can land green. Don't force it into a tracer bullet; sequence it as **expand–contract**. First expand: add the new form beside the old so nothing breaks. Then migrate the call sites over in batches sized by blast radius (per package, per directory), each batch its own ticket blocked by the expand, keeping CI green batch to batch because the old form still exists. Finally contract: delete the old form once no caller remains, in a ticket blocked by every migrate batch. When even the batches can't stay green alone, keep the sequence but let them share an integration branch that all block a final integrate-and-verify ticket; green is promised only there.
 
